                        "entry_id": "c44dee88-e757-3907-93c1-54aa26e2152d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A female storyteller."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "06e544a9-7b49-481d-9ddf-755f4dfb6724",
                                "sense_id": "0f1300c6-e717-3ba6-bb07-c97cef082ee5",
                                "text": "There is a fascinating passage in Margaret Atwood's Surfacing where four Canadians on their way up a river in the wilderness come upon a heron someone preceding them has killed and strung from a tree. Having surveyed its disgusting remains, the raconteuse asks herself \"Why had they strung it up like a lynch victim, why didn't they just throw it away like the trash?\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0d54541d-35e6-44d6-a58e-fce0726c038a",
                                "sense_id": "0f1300c6-e717-3ba6-bb07-c97cef082ee5",
                                "text": "Tom's mother was a superb raconteuse with the enviable gift of painting a verbal canvas of enchanting colours, transporting her audience into a realm of her own making that invariably enhanced the core of the tale."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "99e2fb50-13e1-4f0d-bc79-780c187a002c",
                                "sense_id": "0f1300c6-e717-3ba6-bb07-c97cef082ee5",
                                "text": "So, Shasta had reflected at once, Aunt Lily, by acclamation the family raconteuse, had certainly piqued her concern and puzzlement, if not exactly her surprise."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
